.TH CMDSPECS 1
.SH NAME
cmdspecs \- blackbox testing of Unix command line tools
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cmdspecs
.RB [ \-c
.IR COMMAND ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR TEST ]...\&
.RB [ \-\-timings ]
.RB [ \-k ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-color =\fIWHEN\fR]
.RB [ \-\-version ]
.IR DIR ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cmdspecs
black box tests Unix command line tools.
Given some test scripts, their inputs, and expected outputs,
it verifies that the command line produces the expected output.
If not, it reports problems, and shows the differences.
.PP
Each test case
.I foo
consists of the following files:
.TP
.I foo.script
a script to run the test (this is required)
.TP
.I foo.stdin
the file fed to standard input
.TP
.I foo.stdout
the expected output to the standard output
.TP
.I foo.stderr
the expected output to the standard error
.TP
.I foo.exit
the expected exit code
.TP
.I foo.setup
a script to run before the test
.TP
.I foo.teardown
a script to run after test
.PP
Usually, a single test is not enough. All tests are put into the
same directory, and they may share some setup and teardown code:
.TP
.I setup_once
a script to run once, before any tests
.TP
.I setup
a script to run before each test
.TP
.I teardown
a script to run after each test
.TP
.I teardown_once
a script to run once, after all tests
.PP
The scripts are usually shell scripts, but any executable program
will do.
.B cmdspecs
executes them directly, so they must have the execute permission set,
and a script needs a
.B #!\&
line to choose its interpreter.
.PP
.B cmdspecs
is given the name of the directory with all the tests,
or several such directories.
Each directory has its own setup and teardown scripts,
which are run only for the tests in that directory.
For each directory
.B cmdspecs
does the following:
.PP
.na
.nh
\(bu execute
.I setup_once
.PP
\(bu for each test case (unique prefix 
.IR foo ):
.RS
.HP 2
\(em execute
.I setup
.HP 2
\(em execute 
.I foo.setup
.HP 2
\(em execute the command, by running 
.IR foo.script ,
and redirecting standard input to come from 
.IR foo.stdin ,
and capturing standard output and error and exit codes
.HP 2
\(em execute
.I foo.teardown
.HP 2
\(em execute 
.I teardown
.HP 2
\(em report result of test: does exit code match
.IR foo.exit , 
standard output match 
.IR foo.stdout , 
and standard error match 
.IR foo.stderr ?
.RE
.fi
.hy
.PP
\(bu execute 
.I teardown_once
.PP
Except for
.IR foo.script , 
all of these files are optional. 
If a setup or teardown script is missing, it is simply not executed. 
If one of the standard input, output, or error files is missing, 
it is treated as if it were empty. 
The setup and teardown scripts always get an empty standard input.
If the exit code file is missing, it is treated as if it specified an
exit code of zero.
.PP
A test fails also when one of its setup or teardown scripts fails.
If
.I foo.script
fails or its results do not match the expected ones, both
.I foo.teardown
and
.I teardown
are still run.
If
.I foo.setup
fails,
.I foo.script
and
.I foo.teardown
are not run, but
.I teardown
is.
If
.I setup
fails, none of
.IR foo.setup ,
.IR foo.script ,
.I foo.teardown
and
.I teardown
are run.
If
.I foo.script
is missing or
.I foo.exit
is not valid,
.I foo.setup
and
.I foo.teardown
are not run, but
.I setup
and
.I teardown
are.
If
.I setup_once
fails, no more tests are run and
.I teardown_once
is not run for that directory.
.PP
The scripts may use the following environment variables:
.TP
.B DATADIR
a temporary directory where files may be created by the test.
There is only one such directory for the whole run:
all tests in all directories share it,
and it is not emptied between tests.
It is removed when
.B cmdspecs
exits, unless
.B \-\-keep
is used and a test fails.
.TP
.B TESTNAME
name of the current test (will be empty for 
.B setup_once
and
.BR teardown_once )
.TP
.B SRCDIR
directory from which
.B cmdspecs
was launched
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-command =\fICOMMAND\fR
Ignored for backwards compatibility.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-test =\fITEST\fR
Run only the test
.IR TEST .
Can be given many times.
.TP
.B \-\-timings
Report how long each test takes.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep
Keep temporary data on failure.
The temporary directory is then not removed, and its location is
shown after the test summary on a line starting with
.BR "Test work area:" .
.B DATADIR
is the
.I data
directory in it.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Show the name and result of each test on a line of its own,
instead of a dot for each passed test and
.B F
for each failed one.
.TP
.BR \-\-color =\fIWHEN\fR
Color the output.
.I WHEN
is
.BR auto ,
.BR yes ,
or
.BR no .
The default,
.BR auto ,
colors the output only when it goes to a terminal and the
.B NO_COLOR
environment variable is unset or empty.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Show the version number and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a help message and exit.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
All tests passed.
This is the exit status also when no tests were found.
.TP
.B 1
At least one test failed, a
.I setup_once
or
.I teardown_once
script failed, or the output could not be written.
.TP
.B 2
The command line was invalid:
an unknown option, a directory which could not be read, or a
.B \-\-test
name which matches no test.
No tests are run.
.TP
.B 130
.B cmdspecs
was interrupted.
.SH EXAMPLE
To test that the
.BR echo (1)
command outputs the expected string,
create a file called
.I echo-tests/hello.script
containing the following content:
.IP
.nf
#!/bin/sh
echo hello, world
.fi
.PP
The script must be executable:
.IP
chmod +x echo-tests/hello.script
.PP
Also create the file
.I echo-tests/hello.stdout
containing:
.IP
hello, world
.PP
Then you can run the tests:
.IP
.nf
$ cmdspecs echo-tests
============== test session starts ==============
platform linux \-\- Python 3.13.5, cmdspecs 0.33
srcdir: /home/user
datadir: /tmp/cmdspecs-ab12cd34/data
keep: no
testdirs: echo\-tests
collected 1 test

echo\-tests .                               [100%]
=============== 1 passed in 0.00s ===============
.fi
.PP
The header shows the values of
.B SRCDIR
and
.BR DATADIR ,
whether
.B \-\-keep
is enabled, the test directories and, with
.BR \-\-test ,
the selected tests.
.PP
If you change the stdout file to be something else,
.B cmdspecs
will report the differences:
.IP
.nf
$ cmdspecs echo-tests
============== test session starts ==============
platform linux \-\- Python 3.13.5, cmdspecs 0.33
srcdir: /home/user
datadir: /tmp/cmdspecs-ab12cd34/data
keep: no
testdirs: echo\-tests
collected 1 test

echo\-tests F                               [100%]

=================== FAILURES ====================
_____________________ hello _____________________
stdout diff:
\-\-\- echo\-tests/hello.stdout	2011\-09\-11 19:14:47 +0100
+++ echo\-tests/hello.stdout\-actual	2011\-09\-11 19:14:49 +0100
@@ \-1 +1 @@
\-something else
+hello, world
============ short test summary info ============
FAILED hello \- stdout diff:
=============== 1 failed in 0.00s ===============
.fi
.PP
Furthermore, the 
.I echo-tests
directory will contain the actual output files,
and diffs from the expected files,
for the outputs which differ.
If one of the actual output files is actually correct,
you can actually rename it to be the expected file.
Actually,
that's a very convenient way of creating the expected output files:
you run the test,
fixing things,
until you've manually checked the actual output is correct,
then you rename the file.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR diff (1).
